WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.550
We can all appreciate a grim ending if the
movie calls for it.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.680
But not every film needs to be a downer.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.860
That's a lesson quite a few filmmakers have
learned the hard way.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:13.940
A surprising number of classic films and modern-day
blockbusters initially came equipped with

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:18.560
far bleaker finales, but those sad scenes
were stripped away for something a little

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.029
more lighthearted — or, at least, a little
less depressing.

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:26.080
Here are a few beloved films that originally
had much darker endings.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.839
10 Cloverfield Lane

00:00:27.839 --> 00:00:33.130
While 10 Cloverfield Lane was successful with
audiences and critics alike, many people were

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.590
upset with the film's final moments.

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:39.890
After spending most of the film locked in
a bunker with crazy Howard, Michelle finally

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:44.360
escapes — only to find herself under attack
by aliens.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.700
"Oh, come on!"

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:54.370
It was a complete 180, and a lot of moviegoers
found the ending out of place with the rest

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:55.460
of the movie.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:57.740
But it's got some seriously good undertones.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:03.140
As film critic Tasha Robinson explains, 10
Cloverfield Lane is really a film about domestic

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.930
abuse, and Michelle is a victim who's constantly
running from her problems.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:11.780
Thanks to the controversial ending, Michelle
is able to defeat her adversaries and overcome

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.149
her "habit of running" and the "long-term
effects of abuse."

00:01:15.149 --> 00:01:20.560
The ending is a major victory for Michelle…
and one that wasn't in the original screenplay.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.869
In the first draft of the script — which
was originally titled The Cellar — Michelle

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:29.140
breaks out of the bunker and drives to Chicago,
hoping to find her family.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:33.979
But when she finally arrives, she discovers
the city has been mysteriously destroyed.

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:34.979
The end.

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:39.500
She never knows if the city was obliterated
by aliens or nuked by the Russians, and neither

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:40.500
do we.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.289
The film ends right there, with the Windy
City nothing but a big pile of ashes.

00:01:45.289 --> 00:01:50.479
Robinson calls this an "impersonal and cynical
ending," and evidently the producers at Bad

00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:52.329
Robot thought the same thing.

00:01:52.329 --> 00:01:56.509
After buying the script, they hired Damien
Chazelle — who wrote and directed Whiplash

00:01:56.509 --> 00:02:01.180
and La La Land — to punch up the script
and give Michelle a happier ending in which

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.930
she defeats both her literal and figurative
demons.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:06.859
American Sniper

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:11.980
Clint Eastwood's American Sniper isn't exactly
what you'd call an upbeat movie, but the original

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.880
script took it several notches deeper into
depressing territory.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:20.510
In real-life, Chris Kyle was killed at a shooting
range in February 2013.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:25.080
So since his tragic death was such a pivotal
part of the Chris Kyle story, why didn't it

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.519
make the final cut of Clint Eastwood's movie
about his life?

00:02:28.519 --> 00:02:33.340
Well, according to screenwriter Jason Hall,
his death was supposed to be in the film,

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:38.060
but a few days after the incident, Kyle's
widow called Eastwood and actor Bradley Cooper

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.220
with a favor.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.530
As Hall explained, "[She] called and said,
'This is going to be how my children remember

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.170
their father, so I want you to get it right."

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:50.630
Hoping to honor her request, the creative
group behind the movie considered five different

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:55.110
endings before finally deciding to show Kyle
heading to the infamous shooting range with

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.950
Routh before fading to black.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:01.310
Hall explained the decision by saying, "In
the end, I think we felt that this was a film

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.049
about Chris's life and not about his death."

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:05.049
Training Day

00:03:05.049 --> 00:03:10.930
Denzel Washington picked up his second Oscar
for his performance as Detective Alonzo Harris

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:12.390
in 2001's Training Day.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:16.930
One of the most corrupt cops to ever grace
the silver screen, Harris was declared the

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:20.910
50th greatest villain of all time by the American
Film Institute.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:25.480
"King Kong ain't got s--- on me!"

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.750
So at the end of the film, when Harris is
finally gunned down by the Russian Mafia,

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.569
moviegoers knew he'd gotten his just desserts.

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:37.520
But the original Training Day screenplay actually
had Harris surviving, living to terrorize

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.340
Snoop Dogg another day.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:44.300
After Washington accepted the part, he decided
that Alonzo had to bite the bullet…like,

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:45.860
a whole lot of bullets.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:56.980
In an interview with Dr. Todd Boyd of the
USC School of Cinematic Arts, Washington explained

00:03:56.989 --> 00:03:59.749
that when he read the ending, he "was not
having it."

00:03:59.749 --> 00:04:04.079
Thankfully, he got his wish, and Alonzo got
a glorious death scene.

00:04:04.079 --> 00:04:05.249
True Romance

00:04:05.249 --> 00:04:10.060
Written by Quentin Tarantino, True Romance
tells the story of Clarence, a lonely film

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:13.470
geek who falls in love with a prostitute named
Alabama.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.579
The two end up accidentally getting involved
with the mob.

00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:21.150
At the end of the movie, Clarence and Alabama
find themselves in the middle of a gunfight,

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:24.270
with the Mafia on one side and the cops on
the other.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:28.720
While Clarence does take a bullet, our heroes
manage to take the money and run, settling

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.210
in Mexico and giving audiences an upbeat ending.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.960
However, this wasn't Tarantino's original
vision.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.310
At first, the young screenwriter had Clarence
die in the climactic bloodbath, forcing Alabama

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.150
to grab the money and leave her husband's
body behind.

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:48.060
But director Tony Scott was so enamored with
these two characters that he asked Tarantino

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.240
to let Clarence survive.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:54.370
Tarantino didn't want to make the changes,
so Scott agreed to film two separate endings

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:58.030
— one happy, one sad — and see which one
worked better.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:59.530
No surprise ending here.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:01.680
We got the happy version.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.270
Interstellar

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:07.669
Love it or hate it, you've got to admit Christopher
Nolan's Interstellar is a pretty ambitious

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:11.750
project with a lot of great visuals and some
interesting ideas.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:16.039
Of course, there's also that whole "love is
the one thing that transcends time and space"

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:17.039
bit.

00:05:17.039 --> 00:05:21.020
Some fans appreciated the film's sentimental
ending, while others weren't so crazy about

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.440
the sappy message.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:26.590
And if you're someone who likes your sci-fi
served cold, then you probably would've preferred

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:28.590
the ending in the original screenplay.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:32.940
In the finished film, Coop flies straight
into a black hole, which allows him to view

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.830
the past, present, and future as one moment.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:40.690
Coop uses his fifth-dimensional abilities
to communicate with his daughter and save

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:41.690
mankind.

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:45.610
However, this wasn't what screenwriter Jonathan
Nolan intended.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:49.740
According to Nerdist, the uplifting ending
was his brother Christopher's doing.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:55.389
As the screenwriter explained at a media event,
after Cooper enters the black hole, "the Einstein-Rosen

00:05:55.389 --> 00:06:00.240
bridge [colloquially, a wormhole] (would)
collapse when Cooper tries to send the data

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:01.240
back."

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:05.830
In other words, the guy died, and mankind
most likely slowly starved into extinction.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.069
That's one serious galactic drag.

00:06:08.069 --> 00:06:12.300
And while we're on the subject of narrowly
avoided intergalactic bummers...

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.860
Guardians of the Galaxy

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:18.830
Generally speaking, the Marvel Cinematic Universe
usually likes to keep things upbeat at the

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:20.070
ends of its movies.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:24.009
And perhaps the happiest ending of all comes
in Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:28.650
After defeating Ronan the Accuser, Star-Lord
plugs a new Awesome Mixtape into the ship's

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:33.430
tape deck and "Ain't No Mountain High Enough"
plays over a feel-good montage of all the

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:35.000
good guys from the film.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:39.140
It's an ending specifically designed to let
moviegoers leave with a smile.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:42.610
Sure, things got super sad when Star-Lord
read that letter from his mom.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.930
But at least he's got a new family now, right?

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:47.509
You know who's still sad and alone, though?

00:06:47.509 --> 00:06:49.190
Peter Quill's grandfather.

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:53.659
Originally, the old man was supposed to show
up in the film's final moments, which would've

00:06:53.659 --> 00:06:56.300
totally killed the Marvin Gaye mood.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:57.909
Director James Gunn told SlashFilm:

00:06:57.909 --> 00:07:03.230
"It was Grandpa Quill and he has this photograph
of Meredith and Peter as a little boy and

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.520
he looks up at the stars and we go up to the
stars and it was really sweet.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:10.830
It means that he must have seen Quill getting
abducted at the end of that day and is still

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:12.319
waiting for him to return.

00:07:12.319 --> 00:07:14.479
But it was freaking sad so we took it out."

00:07:14.479 --> 00:07:18.900
Grandpa Quill holding a photo of his dead
daughter, still waiting for his grandson to

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.879
come back home after all those years?

00:07:20.879 --> 00:07:22.690
Yeah, talk about a buzzkill.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:24.340
Cue dancing Baby Groot!

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:35.540
Thanks for watching!

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:38.440
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:41.080
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

